{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1479,
    "dDurationMs": 3891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicists love particles, and with good\nreason – almost everything in the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5370,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is made up of particles, so the physicist’s\napproach to understanding the universe is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8820,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to understand the particles that make it up.\nIf you want to discover and catalogue new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12629,
    "dDurationMs": 4221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles (which physicists do), here are\nthree approaches you can take."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16850,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, you can take old particles we already\nknow stuff about and just stick them together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20710,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to build new *composite* particles. That’s\nwhat chemists and molecular biologists spend"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24890,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of their time doing, and it’s kind\nof like figuring out what you can build with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28320,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "legos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29320,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Second, you can smash old particles together\nwith ever increasing violence, either hoping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33280,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to break an old particle apart into previously\nunknown constituents or to excite a new particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37961,
    "dDurationMs": 4578,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into existence from the quantum fields that\nunderly all reality. Sometimes this works"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42539,
    "dDurationMs": 4961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the smashing reveals an entirely new *fundamental*\nbuilding block of the universe like quarks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47500,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the Higgs boson. But most of the time it\njust makes a big mess, an explosion of old"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52660,
    "dDurationMs": 1390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles we already know about."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54050,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Third, you can put old particles in new environments\nso that they behave differently, or put old"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59200,
    "dDurationMs": 3839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles together in new ways so that new\nparticle-like behaviors emerge through their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63039,
    "dDurationMs": 1401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "*collective* quantum interactions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64440,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, in certain crystals, the particles\nthat move around are not electrons themselves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69290,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but in fact holes or gaps in a densely packed\nsea of electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73210,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or, when you make certain materials really\nreally cold, free electrons in them stop acting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77580,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like electrons and team up in pairs to act\nlike weird electron-only atoms that move around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82500,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with essentially no resistance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84020,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or when you make certain metals really cold,\nelectrons in them start acting as if they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88070,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were 1000 times *heavier* than normal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or if you make a super thin essentially 2-dimensional\nsheet of gallium arsenide with a perpendicular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95080,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic field and parallel electric field\nand make it really cold, electrons start behaving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99450,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as if they had an electric charge that’s\na fraction of what they normally do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103170,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or if you force electrons into a special 1-dimensional\nline, the particles that move back and forth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107440,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "along that line *look* like electrons, except\nseparated so that some of them have the charge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112070,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of an electron but no spin, while others have\nthe spin but no charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116560,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or if you cool helium down super super cold,\nyou’ll find emergent particles that behave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120890,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like higgs bosons! These emergent higgs bosons\nwere first discovered in 1973, 40 years before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127500,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the higgs boson fundamental particle was discovered\nat the Large Hadron Collider by violently"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131770,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smashing together protons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132950,
    "dDurationMs": 3909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, the particles that emerge when\nyou put collections of electrons together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136859,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in materials aren’t fundamental constituents\nof the universe, but they’re far more diverse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141489,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and bizarre and weird and cool. And unlike\nsearching for fundamental particles where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146269,
    "dDurationMs": 4481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you just have to wait and see what nature\nhas in store, we can actively find and curate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150750,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new emergent particles simply by making different\nweird materials that allow their emergent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155170,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties to come to life. Plus, materials\nwith emergent particles have real and far-reaching"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160019,
    "dDurationMs": 5351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "practical technological use: in electronics,\ncomputer chips, levitating high speed trains,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165370,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even the magnets and detectors used to\ndiscover the higgs boson at the Large Hadron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169680,
    "dDurationMs": 3539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Collider."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173219,
    "dDurationMs": 4321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This video was supported in part by the Gordon\nand Betty Moore Foundation’s Emergent Phenomena"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177540,
    "dDurationMs": 4899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Quantum Systems Initiative. EPiQS supports\ndiscovery-driven research on novel electronic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182439,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "materials and aims to stimulate breakthroughs\nto fundamentally change our understanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186409,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the organizing principles of complex matter.\nTo learn more, visit Moore.org or follow the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191319,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moore foundation on twitter."
    } ]
  } ]
}
